      "title": "Ben Roberts-Smith collaborates with gym business run by influencer that sold ‘war crimes’ pre-workout supplement",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/ben-roberts-smith-collaborates-gym-business-influencer-war-crimes-pre-workout-supplement-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Zero Limits supplements is co-owned by rightwing podcaster and fitness coach Chris Katelaris, also known as ‘Big Chocky’</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Ben Roberts-Smith has collaborated with a gym supplements company run by a rightwing influencer which recently sold a pre-workout product called “war crimes”.<strong> </strong></p><p>Roberts-Smith, one of Australia’s most decorated living soldiers who is currently awaiting trial on war crimes charges, has recently launched <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/16/ben-roberts-smith-launches-brand-epic-1-two-amid-legal-battle-ntwnfb\">an apparel brand, Epic 1 Two, producing T-shirts and gym wear</a>.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/ben-roberts-smith-collaborates-gym-business-influencer-war-crimes-pre-workout-supplement-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-07T00:58:11"
    },
    {
      "title": "When a single vowel stands in the path to victory, the stakes could not be higher for these spelling bee champions",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/when-a-single-vowel-stands-in-the-path-to-victory-the-stakes-could-not-be-higher-for-these-spelling-bee-champions",
      "description": "<p>Primary school students bested 200,000 competitors across New South Wales to reach the Premier’s Spelling Bee finals in Sydney</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Competing in a spelling bee is not for the faint of heart. Sometimes, the difference between victory and loss is one incorrect vowel and you’re undone, months of training reduced to nought.</p><p>At a small theatre hall in Sydney’s west on Friday afternoon, 34 primary school students sit stony-faced on a brightly lit stage, knowing this pressure only too well.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/when-a-single-vowel-stands-in-the-path-to-victory-the-stakes-could-not-be-higher-for-these-spelling-bee-champions\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-06T23:06:10"
    },
    {
      "title": "Anti-fire ant eradication activist convicted after using legal arguments ‘similar to sovereign citizens’",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/trevor-hold-activist-convicted-qld-fire-ant-eradication-program-queensland-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Legal expert compares Trevor Hold’s failed defence to ‘tried and tested argument’ used by sovereign citizen movement</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>The Queensland leader of a group opposing the eradication of fire ants in Australia has been convicted of obstructing government officers, after a magistrate threw out his argument that the commonwealth-funded program is illegal.</p><p>Trevor Hold was fined $1,500 but a conviction was not recorded. Hold has described himself as an “ultrapreneur”, a mental health counsellor, an organic cattle farmer and the chief executive officer of the anti-program group the Australian Advocacy Group – and as self-employed.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/trevor-hold-activist-convicted-qld-fire-ant-eradication-program-queensland-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-06T14:00:46"
    },
    {
      "title": "The Victorian Liberals’ campaign bus was supposed to be a reset. Instead it became the symbol of a rough week",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/the-victorian-liberals-campaign-bus-was-supposed-to-be-a-reset-instead-it-became-the-symbol-of-a-rough-week",
      "description": "<p>Crashing into a shop awning ended the first outing of the Jess Express – but the opposition’s electoral momentum will be harder to stop</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Jess Wilson has barely put a foot wrong since becoming Victorian Liberal leader a little under a year ago. That was until this week, which ended with <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/06/liberal-jess-wilson-campaign-bus-crashes-into-shop-first-day-victorian-election\">her campaign bus reversing into a small business</a>.</p><p>The bus was launched on Friday, a few days earlier than planned, in what appeared to be an effort to reset Wilson’s relationship with the Spring Street press pack.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/07/the-victorian-liberals-campaign-bus-was-supposed-to-be-a-reset-instead-it-became-the-symbol-of-a-rough-week\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-06T14:00:45"
    },
    {
      "title": "Death a ‘distinct possibility’ in collision running and face-slapping competitions, inquiry hears",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/06/run-nation-power-slap-combat-sports-safety-inquiry-nsw",
      "description": "<p>Impacts in emerging contact sports are akin to coward punches and cannot be made safer with regulation, neurologist says</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Collision running and face-slapping competitions have similar impacts to that of a coward punch or “king-hit”, with death a “distinct possibility”, a concussion expert has told a parliamentary inquiry.</p><p>A New South Wales inquiry is examining whether emerging competitions such as Run Nation, in which two players run at each other and collide at high speed, and Power Slap, where players take turns slapping each other across the face, should be considered combat sports to better regulate the safety of participants.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/06/run-nation-power-slap-combat-sports-safety-inquiry-nsw\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-06T08:23:10"
    }
